Machine code (binary 1s and 0s) is the ultimate foundation that all software runs on, but C is the primary programming language used to build almost all modern languages. [1, 2, 3] 
## The Foundation: Machine Code and Assembly

* Hardware level: CPU chips only understand binary.
* Assembly language: A human-readable version of binary.
* The bridge: Every programming language must eventually convert into this code. [4, 5, 6, 7] 

## The Mother of Modern Languages: C
Developed in 1972, C is the root of modern software. It was used to build the operating systems (like Unix, Windows, and Linux) that other languages run on. [8, 9, 10, 11, 12] 
Many popular languages are directly built using C or heavily inspired by its syntax: [13, 14] 

* Python: The standard version (CPython) is written in C.
* JavaScript: The engines that run JS (like Google's V8) are written in C++.
* C++ and C#: Direct descendants designed to add new features to C.
* Java: The original Java Virtual Machine (JVM) was written in C. [15, 16, 17, 18, 19] 

## The Language Tree Structure

[ Machine Code / Binary ]
          │
  [ Assembly Language ]
          │
        [ C ]
   ───────┴───────
  │       │       │
[C++]  [Java]  [Python]

